\title{RV-3A: adversarial review of eager reduction in place (package MO-3), from the source}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Review "RV-3A" of 2026-11-25 (02:03 to 02:55 UTC of the following UTC day). Its subject is the patch of [package MO-3](rep-MO-3): the comparison of an \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} argument runs in place under the mode bit, pair entries whose answer can depend on the mode are local to their declaration, setting \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=local|shared|deferred~} with \code{local} proposed as the default. The reviewer did not write the patch and was asked to refute it, working from the source: the patch read line by line against the official kernel (\code{\verb~|src/kernel/type_checker.cpp~} of Lean 4.36.0, commit \code{611c117b}), every suspicion turned into an export, and every export judged by the official checker (leanchecker 4.36.0, \code{\verb~|--from-export~}, in the project's Docker image without network). A second reviewer attacked the same build as a black box; this report does not cover that work. Nothing here is a timing.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Result in one page}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdict: NO-GO for \code{local} as the default}, and \code{shared} is no fallback. The gated build of MO-3 rejects, with default options and through both launchers, \strong{ten exports that the official checker accepts} and that the published build accepts; seven of them use only the arena's prelude. With a rejection of the machine final, each of them is a wrong verdict of the product.}
\li{\strong{No accept of a statement that is not derivable was found} (kind F), and the reading of the patch gives an argument why this change cannot produce one: every true answer it adds is a conversion that one of the two sound orders, an entry of the pair table for the same two expressions, or the plain comparison of the K rule licenses (section 5).}
\li{\strong{Finding F1} (wrong reject, honest prelude, three exports): a comparison of plain mode that a shortcut answers does not make the sub-comparisons that the official kernel makes and caches; when such a sub-pair is asked under the bit in the same declaration, the official success cache answers and the machine computes in eager order. The run alone before a rejection (rule 7 of MO-3) repeats the same shortcut. Shown through a skipped proof position, a skipped unread position, and an argument comparison that is refuted before its last arguments are compared.}
\li{\strong{Finding F2} (wrong reject, honest prelude, two exports): the search by expression (rule 5) is bounded by the structural walk it calls, 40 host frames and 256 nodes; a cached pair under other records beyond either bound is not found. This is the risk that the report of MO-3 lists as "not demonstrated".}
\li{\strong{Finding F3} (a Nat function whose body does not compute; three wrong rejects in the default set, two more under another switch set, one accept of an official reject that is new with the patch, kind T): the memo of a K reduction is written in one mode and read in the other. Rule 6 of MO-3 makes a recursor reduce under the bit whenever it reduces in plain mode; it does not cover a K comparison that is true under the bit only, and the claim "no memo word needs a stamp" does not hold there.}
\li{\strong{Finding F4} (wrong reject, honest prelude, closed terms, two exports): the plain mode reduces a K recursor through the same-head shortcut where the official kernel leaves it stuck in both modes; under the bit the machine runs without the shortcut, leaves a second recursor stuck without any eager-only event, so neither rule 6 nor rule 7 runs, and compares the reduct of the first with the stuck second.}
\li{\strong{The published build}: one wrong reject of its deferred mechanism was found on the way (an export of the F3 family: the pair first under the bit, then in plain mode, in one binder), and five accepts of official rejects in plain mode that belong to the accepted risk of the switch \code{lazyeval}; all need a Nat function whose body does not compute (section 4).}
\li{\strong{Attacked without result}: the places where the bit is set, saved and restored (two check sites, suspension, cut, the K rule), the stamp logic of local entries, the host depth bound (six bounds), the session cap, worker counts, pipelined loading, a second pass, a checked term reused by a later declaration, and a pair that is true under the bit only with honest definitions (the real \code{\verb~|Nat.gcd~}, \code{\verb~|Nat.land~}, \code{\verb~|Nat.xor~}, \code{\verb~|Nat.shiftRight~} of Init): section 6.}
\li{\strong{Repairs}: two changes of two lines were tried in a review copy; they repair F1 and F4 on the rows of this review and move no other verdict of 159 rows. F2 needs a complete search or a decline; F3 touches the design, the memo of a reduction that depends on a comparison (section 7).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Conditions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The candidate}: the gated build of MO-3, a development distribution of its patched private tree (jars \code{c5814eaec85b}): the executable, a GraalVM Community Edition native image, and the JVM launcher. \strong{The base}: the build of the pristine twin of that tree (jars \code{3babbe8de8f3}), which is the main tree of 22:46 UTC with the package on the re-check, so a rejection of the machine is final in both. Default options means no \code{-X} option; a setting is named where one was used.}
\li{\strong{The official verdict} of every export named here: leanchecker of Lean 4.36.0 (commit \code{611c117bfaf7}, aarch64) with \code{\verb~|--from-export~} inside the project's arena image, without network; exit status 0 is accept, 1 is reject. The verdict files (hash of the export, verdict, full output) are handed over with the exports.}
\li{\strong{The exports} are built with the hostile suite's builder (\src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/gen/lk.py}) on the arena's prelude, with the building blocks of the suite's eager family: \code{\verb~|X = Nat.add (G intro) 0~} and \code{\verb~|Y p = Nat.add (G p) 0~}, a pair that is true in plain mode (same head, the proofs by proof irrelevance) and false under the flag (the closed side folds first). Twelve exports are built on the real Init instead (the export of the Init corpus, read lazily), for the definitions the arena's prelude does not have.}
\li{\strong{Verdicts of the machine} are exit statuses of the launchers: 0 accept, 1 reject, 2 decline. "Wave 6 with eager" is the default set without \code{skipargs} and \code{congruence}, given with \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches~}.}
\li{\strong{An instrumented copy} of the patched tree (JVM launcher only; a trace of comparisons, pair writes and events behind a system property) was used to confirm causes. It was never used for a verdict that is reported.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The official source against section 1 of the report of MO-3}

\p{Section 1 of [the report of MO-3](rep-MO-3) is right in what it says: the flag is set in \code{\verb~|infer_app~} in check mode around the one comparison, after the type of the argument was inferred; it is read in \code{\verb~|lazy_delta_reduction~} and in \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_core~}; the caches belong to one type checker and are shared by the modes. Read against the source, five facts are missing from it or are not followed by the patch. Each of them is the root of a finding below.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Which comparisons enter the success cache.} Only the wrapper \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} caches, and it is the wrapper that the argument loops call: \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_args~} and \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_app~} for every argument pair, \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_proof_irrel~} for the two TYPES of two proofs, \code{\verb~|try_eta_struct_core~}, \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_binding~}. So a plain comparison fills the cache with its sub-pairs, the pair of types of two proofs among them, and a later comparison under the flag is answered by any of them before anything is computed. A machine that answers the outer comparison by a shortcut has none of these entries (finding F1).}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|is_def_eq_args~} compares the last arguments first} and stops at the first false pair; the pairs it compared before that one are cached although the argument comparison as a whole fails (finding F1, third export).}
\li{\strong{The comparison of the K rule runs under the flag} when the reduction is asked from under the flag: the flag is a field, \code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~} calls \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~}, and nothing clears it. The report says so. What it does not draw from it: the answer can be true under the flag and false in plain mode as well as the reverse, as soon as a Nat function folds to something its unfolding does not reach (finding F3).}
\li{\strong{A recursor application is not in the cache of \code{\verb~|whnf_core~}.} Both ends of the recursor case return before the insertion, and the first \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} of \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_core~} runs with \code{\verb~|cheap_proj~} and caches nothing. So the official kernel decides the K rule again every time, from its success cache or by computing in the mode of the moment; "stuck" is never remembered, and a reduct only through \code{\verb~|whnf~}. The machine remembers both for the session (findings F3 and F4).}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|whnf~} folds Nat functions without looking at free variables}; only the fold of \code{\verb~|lazy_delta_reduction~} is guarded. So the official plain loop, which unfolds one step at a time, does not fold an open application, while one reduction to weak head normal form does. With honest definitions the two agree; with a body that does not compute they do not (section 4, the note on the published build).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Findings: wrong rejects with the arena's prelude}

\p{All exports of this section: official verdict \strong{accept}; candidate with default options \strong{reject}, exit status 1, through the executable and through the JVM launcher, with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers; base build \strong{accept} through both launchers; \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} accepts; \code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~} reports the disagreement and declines.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Finding}\th{Export}\th{\code{local}}\th{\code{shared}}\th{\code{deferred}}\th{wave 6 with eager}}
\tr{\td{F1}\td{\code{rv3a-hidden-proofpos-types-then-eager-ok}}\td{reject}\td{accept}\td{accept}\td{accept}}
\tr{\td{F1}\td{\code{rv3a-hidden-unread-arg-then-eager-ok}}\td{reject}\td{reject}\td{accept}\td{accept}}
\tr{\td{F1}\td{\code{rv3a-hidden-refuted-sibling-then-eager-ok}}\td{reject}\td{reject}\td{accept}\td{reject}}
